         <bibl xml:id="work-5511" type="lawd:ConceptualWork">
            <title xml:id="name-5511-1" xml:lang="en" srophe:tags="#syriaca-headword">The Fourth Catechetical Homily— <foreign xml:lang="syr" source="bib5511-3">ܡܐܡܪܐ ܕܐܪܒܥܐ</foreign></title>
            <author ref="http://syriaca.org/person/780" xml:lang="en" source="bib5511-2">Theodore of Mopsuestia — <foreign xml:lang="syr" source="bib5511-3">ܬܐܕܘܪܘܤ ܡܦܫܩܢܐ</foreign>
            </author>
            <idno type="URI">http://syriaca.org/work/5511</idno>
            <textLang mainLang="syr"/>
            <date notBefore="0392" notAfter="0428" source="bib5511-6">392-428 CE</date>
            <noteGrp type="abstract">
               <desc xml:lang="en">Abstract</desc>
               <note corresp="http://syriaca.org/nhsl" xml:lang="en" type="abstract" source="bib5511-9">
                  Discusses the creedal statements about the divinity of the Son. “Born of His Father before all the worlds” means that the Son is eternal and consubstantial with the Father and, therefore, that He is not a creature. “True God of true God”: the one who is of the same nature with God the Father is truly God. “Consubstantial with the Father”: the Son is born of the Father, He is true God as the Father is, and He is consubstantial with the Father. Although the term “consubstantial” is not scriptural, Theodore employs a number of quotations from Scripture (Jn 1:1; Jn 10: 30; Jn 10: 27-28; Jn 10: 29; Jn 14: 9, 11; Mt 11: 27) in order to corroborate the consubstantiality of the Son. The last creedal statement “By whom the worlds were made and all things were created” indicates the divinity of the Son. 
               </note>
            </noteGrp>
